;; projects/conformance/test-fixtures/sim-configs/full-capability.edn -- the
;; full-capability gate loop's own --config passthrough vehicle (M6's
;; reference-picture baseline, notes/ADRs.md ADR-0015): every capability
;; sim's own docs/demos/ has separately demonstrated, exercised together
;; in ONE corpus, so the v2 gate judges the full breadth of what sim
;; produces rather than the plain default pathway's ADT-only slice
;; (projects/conformance/test-fixtures/reports/sim-v2-gate-baseline.edn's own
;; legacy-floor scope). Designed for --patients 60 (this file's own
;; explicit ordinals below only cover 40..59; fewer patients than that
;; simply leaves part of the module-only cohort unrealized, not an error).
;;
;; TWO COHORTS, not one population wearing both hats -- ordinals 40..59
;; are pulled out of the CBC pool below by an EXPLICIT :patient-ordinal
;; override in BOTH :pathways (an EMPTY pathway) and :module-assignment
;; (sinusitis). This split is load-bearing, not stylistic: a module's own
;; compiled trajectory is PREPENDED ahead of a patient's authored pathway
;; at execution time (ehr-testing-sim.engine's :registered decide method,
;; `:prepend-steps`), so a patient given BOTH an authored :admission step
;; (the CBC pathway below) AND a module whose own compiled encounter also
;; opens (or closes) an encounter would carry TWO encounter-opening events
;; for one patient -- illegal under this project's single-encounter-
;; horizon invariant (`admission-only-when-new`/`outpatient-visit-only-
;; when-new`, ADR-0007 point 3's "a single encounter, not a lifelong
;; history" scope), confirmed empirically this session (a combined
;; pathway+module attempt on the SAME patient population made every run
;; :self-check-failed). `ehr-testing-sim.engine_test.clj`'s own "module-
;; only patients" fixture uses this identical empty-pathway-plus-module
;; pattern; this config just gives it a name and a cohort size.
;;
;; Cohort 1 -- ordinals 0..39 (the :pathways pool's fallback, no explicit
;; override, so every ordinal NOT named in the override list below draws
;; it): an order-bearing authored pathway, the CBC order/result cycle per
;; sim's own resources/order-profiles.edn -- the SAME shape
;; docs/demos/order-result/config.edn uses. `:module-assignment` has no
;; pool entry of its own (every entry below is a `:patient-ordinal`
;; override), so `assign-module` legitimately resolves to nil for this
;; whole cohort (its own docstring's "most people don't have chronic
;; sinusitis" case) -- no module ever touches them. Churn (turned on by
;; the caller's own --churn flag, not this file -- see below) has a full
;; admission/order/discharge pathway to insert into, so this cohort is
;; also where ADT^A11/A12/A13/A17/A40 (cancel-admit/cancel-transfer/
;; cancel-discharge/bed-swap/merge) actually have gaps to land in.
;;
;; Cohort 2 -- ordinals 40..59 (listed explicitly, below): an EMPTY
;; authored pathway plus the M5b-vendored sinusitis module
;; (resources/modules/sinusitis.json), assigned by the SAME explicit-
;; ordinal override `ehr-testing-sim.engine/assign-module` resolves before
;; ever consulting a pool. The module's own compiled trajectory (per
;; docs/gmf-interpreter.md's own outpatient sketch) is this cohort's
;; entire record: `:outpatient-visit`/`:outpatient-visit-end` (ADT^A04,
;; no closing message -- real ambulatory feeds send a single A04) plus
;; whatever medication/observation IR the module's own walk produces.
;; `:module-horizon-days` 3650 (10 years) matches the value
;; `ehr-testing-sim`'s own `engine_test.clj` module-assignment fixtures
;; use -- wide enough that the module's walk isn't truncated by the
;; horizon before it produces visible clinical content.
;;
;; :churn-profile -- the harness still passes the bare --churn flag (so
;; "churn on" is a real, visible CLI toggle, not something buried in a
;; fixture), but `run-command`'s own `effective-churn-profile` resolution
;; checks a caller-supplied `:churn-profile` BEFORE the bare `:churn`
;; boolean (`(cond churn-profile ... churn churn/sample-profile ...)`),
;; so this file's own explicit profile below wins over --churn's default
;; `churn/sample-profile` without the two conflicting -- both keys reach
;; `run-command` together (:churn from the flag, :churn-profile from this
;; file), and only the latter's rates are actually used. The rates below
;; are elevated well above `churn/sample-profile`'s own 0.01-0.03
;; ("modest, illustrative") specifically so this REFERENCE corpus has a
;; real chance at the full churn trigger family (ADT^A11/A12/A13/A17/A40)
;; at a patient count still small enough to read -- an empirical finding
;; from this session's own trial runs: at `sample-profile`'s default
;; rates and 40 CBC-cohort patients, only cancel-admit and merge actually
;; fired (1 each); cancel-transfer/cancel-discharge/bed-swap never did.
;; This is a deliberate authoring choice for a reference fixture, not a
;; claim that real hospital churn runs this hot.
;;
;; No :site-profile here -- deliberately: the gate should judge sim's
;; STANDARD dialect (the same default MSH/PV1/code-table rendering every
;; other loop in this repo already sees), not a site profile's own
;; declared surfaces. A dialect-variant loop (gating the SAME corpus
;; rendered under a second site profile, per docs/demos/site-profiles/)
;; is a candidate future addition -- explicitly NOT this session's scope.
{:pathways
 [{:pathway {:name "cbc-order"
             :steps [{:type :admission :location "Renal"}
                     {:type :order :profile :cbc}
                     {:type :discharge}]}
   :weight 1}
  {:patient-ordinal 40 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
  {:patient-ordinal 41 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
  {:patient-ordinal 42 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
